from c8e_base import *
LB(9,1,'Language work: binomials, prefixes, learning idioms, past perfect, a letter to the editor','Language work for How I Taught My Grandmother to Read: word pairs, prefixes, idioms and the past perfect','Words and sentences for a story about learning',
 "In the story the writer teaches her grandmother to read, and the language work of the chapter helps us talk about learning. We study word pairs called binomials, prefixes that change meanings, idioms about studying, the difference between the simple past and the past perfect, and the format of a formal letter to the editor.",
 [("A binomial is a pair of words joined by 'and' or 'or' that always appear in the same order, such as sink or swim, on and off, mix and match, all or nothing, part and parcel, pick and choose, sooner or later and by leaps and bounds.","binomials"),
  ("Sink or swim means you must succeed by your own effort or fail. Part and parcel means an essential part of something. Pick and choose means to select carefully. Sooner or later means at some time in the future. By leaps and bounds means very fast progress.","meanings of the binomials"),
  ("A prefix is a group of letters placed before a word to change its meaning. The prefix un makes the opposite, as in unable and unhappy. Im also gives the opposite before p or m, as in impossible and immature.","prefixes un and im"),
  ("Dis gives the opposite or removal, as in disagree and disappear. In gives the opposite, as in incorrect and inactive. Mis means wrongly, as in misunderstand and misspell. Extra means beyond, as in extraordinary and extracurricular.","prefixes dis, in, mis, extra"),
  ("The book also gives idioms about learning. To hit the books means to study hard. To draw a blank means to be unable to remember something. To learn the ropes means to learn how a job is done.","idioms: hit the books, draw a blank, learn the ropes"),
  ("To rack one's brain means to think very hard. To learn by heart means to memorise something so well that you can repeat it exactly. To burn the midnight oil means to work or study late into the night.","idioms: rack one's brain, learn by heart, burn the midnight oil"),
  ("The simple past tense talks about a finished action at a definite time in the past, as in 'The writer taught her grandmother every evening'.","simple past"),
  ("The past perfect tense is made with had plus the third form of the verb. It shows the action that happened first when two past actions are told together. In 'When the teacher arrived, the lesson had started', the starting came first and the arriving came second.","past perfect: had + past participle"),
  ("To fill a blank, ask which action happened earlier. Put the past perfect for the earlier action and the simple past for the later one, as in 'She had finished her work before her friends came'.","choosing the right tense"),
  ("A letter to the editor is a formal letter about a matter of public interest. It has the writer's name and address, the date, a subject of four or five words in title case, the salutation Sir or Madam, a body of at least three paragraphs and the close Yours truly.","format of a letter to the editor"),
  ("In the body, the first paragraph states the problem and refers to a news item or an experience. The second paragraph explains the causes and the effects. The third paragraph offers solutions and asks the editor to publish the letter.","three paragraphs"),
  ("For the speaking activity in the book, you talk about a turncoat, a person who changes sides to suit their own gain, and use phrases such as 'I strongly believe' and 'to put it in a few words'. For the listening task on digital literacy, read the questions first and listen for the exact words.","speaking and listening")],
 ("Complete the sentence with the correct tense: 'When I reached the station, the train ____ (leave).'",
  [("When I reached the station, the train had left.","The train left first, so the past perfect had left is used."),
   ("Add the prefix mis to the word 'spell'.","Misspell means to spell wrongly.")]),
 ("Explain the difference between the simple past and the past perfect with one example each.",
  ["the simple past is for a finished action in the past","the past perfect is for the earlier of two past actions","it is made with had plus the third form of the verb","a correct example sentence for each"],
  ["Using the past perfect when only one past action is mentioned","Using had with the first form of the verb"]),
 ["Binomials: sink or swim, part and parcel, pick and choose, sooner or later, by leaps and bounds.","Prefixes: un, im, dis, in, mis, extra.","Idioms: hit the books, draw a blank, learn the ropes, rack one's brain, learn by heart, burn the midnight oil.","Past perfect = had + past participle, for the earlier past action.","A letter to the editor has three paragraphs: problem, analysis, solution."],
 [mcq("k1a-1",1,"Which pair is a binomial?","sooner or later",[("quickly and slowly","These are not a fixed pair expression."),("read and book","This is not a fixed phrase.")],"Sooner or later is a fixed pair joined by or."),
  mcq("k1a-2",1,"'To hit the books' means","to study hard",[("to throw books away","This is a literal and wrong reading."),("to buy many books","The idiom is about studying.")],"Hit the books means study hard."),
  mcq("k1a-3",1,"Which prefix makes the opposite of 'possible'?","im",[("un","Unpossible is not a word."),("mis","Mis means wrongly.")],"Impossible takes im before p."),
  mcq("k1a-4",1,"The prefix 'mis' in 'misunderstand' means","wrongly",[("again","Again is the meaning of re."),("beyond","Beyond is the meaning of extra.")],"Mis means wrongly."),
  mcq("k1a-5",1,"'Part and parcel' means","an essential part of something",[("a parcel sent by post","This is a literal reading."),("a small piece only","The phrase means a necessary part.")],"Part and parcel means a necessary part."),
  mcq("k1a-6",2,"'I rack my brain' means that I","think very hard",[("feel angry","The idiom is about thinking."),("forget everything","This is draw a blank.")],"To rack one's brain is to think hard."),
  mcq("k1a-7",2,"'When we reached, the film ___.' Choose the past perfect.","had started",[("started","This is the simple past."),("has started","This is the present perfect.")],"Had started is the past perfect."),
  mcq("k1a-8",2,"'She ___ her work before her friends came.' The best form is","had finished",[("finishes","This is the present."),("will finish","This is the future.")],"The finishing came first, so use had finished."),
  mcq("k1a-9",2,"Which idiom means to work late at night?","burn the midnight oil",[("learn the ropes","This means to learn how a job is done."),("draw a blank","This means to be unable to remember.")],"Burn the midnight oil means study or work late."),
  mcq("k1a-10",2,"Which word is made with the prefix 'dis'?","disagree",[("distance","Distance does not use dis as a prefix with a base word."),("display","Display is not formed by dis plus play meaning the opposite.")],"Disagree means not agree."),
  mcq("k1a-11",3,"How many paragraphs should the body of a letter to the editor have, at least?","three",[("one","The book asks for at least three."),("two","The book asks for at least three.")],"Problem, analysis and solution."),
  mcq("k1a-12",3,"What does the third paragraph of a letter to the editor contain?","solutions and a request to publish",[("only the date","The date is at the top."),("the statement of the problem","This is paragraph one.")],"Paragraph three offers solutions.")])
